import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CDpkVv68.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Character progression","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"game-design/characters/progression.md","filePath":"game-design/characters/progression.md"}'),n={name:"game-design/characters/progression.md"};function s(o,e,l,c,d,h){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="character-progression" tabindex="-1">Character progression <a class="header-anchor" href="#character-progression" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Character progression&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Design status:</strong> Proposed; open questions remain Unresolved. Referenced Accepted decisions retain their existing scope. <strong>Implementation/availability:</strong> no changes to gameplay or runtime configuration; this redesign is not established as live. <strong>Verification:</strong> documentation review, not a playtest; current availability is not established here. <strong>Reviewed:</strong> 2026-10-09 for public projection; design status and mechanics preserved from the canonical specification.</p><p><a href="/Syrolands-Public/guide/characters/progression.html">Player-facing overview</a> · <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/progression.html">Character progression</a></p><h2 id="purpose-and-ownership" tabindex="-1">Purpose and ownership <a class="header-anchor" href="#purpose-and-ownership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Purpose and ownership&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>A low-skill character is inexperienced, not nonfunctional.</strong> Syrolands should be fun from the first day. Repetitive training should not be a prerequisite for useful participation; routine low-skill misses and sluggish early combat are problems to avoid, not a runtime diagnosis made by this documentation task. Skill 20 should already let a character participate meaningfully.</p><p>Higher skill expands capability, consistency, difficulty envelope and mastery. It should not merely make an unusable activity finally usable. This page owns the general progression philosophy; <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/skills-and-skillcap.html">Skills &amp; Skillcap</a> owns ranges, gain and teaching, <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/techniques-and-specialization.html">Techniques</a> owns the skill/knowledge/path hierarchy, <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/character-creation.html">Character creation</a> owns starting histories, and <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/stats.html">Stats</a> owns responsiveness and stat direction.</p><p>The <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/professions/profession-system.html">Profession System</a> retains craft-specific discovery, journals, sharing, production and commissions. Its existing entry/progression anchors link to these shared character rules rather than maintain a second gain table.</p><h2 id="character-skill-knowledge-and-player-mastery" tabindex="-1">Character skill, knowledge and player mastery <a class="header-anchor" href="#character-skill-knowledge-and-player-mastery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Character skill, knowledge and player mastery&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Skill determines capability. Knowledge determines possibility/information. Player mastery determines performance.</strong> Knowledge creates choices; skill improves execution. These three axes continue the Batch 01 model.</p><p>Two players with equal skill can perform differently through human execution. Two GM crafters can know different techniques and materials. A person who knows a good procedure may still need sufficient character capability to execute it reliably. Learning a fact externally does not automatically grant character knowledge or technical proficiency.</p><h2 id="specialization-without-one-optimal-template" tabindex="-1">Specialization without one optimal template <a class="header-anchor" href="#specialization-without-one-optimal-template" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Specialization without one optimal template&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The approximate 700-point budget should support three to five primary specializations, supporting skills around 60–90, and lower-investment utility, all within the actual cap. Neither exactly seven GM skills nor fourteen skills at 50 should become the universal optimal answer.</p><p>Low/mid investment gives useful options. Deep investment grants distinct capabilities, consistency and access to harder situations, so spreading points too widely has real opportunity cost. Mastery remains valuable without being necessary for every endgame path.</p><p><strong>Illustration, not a canonical build:</strong> a Hunter could combine Archery, Tracking, Animal Lore and Anatomy with Tactics, Tailoring, Healing and Camping at different non-100 values. No numerical allocation or mandatory skill package is approved by this example. The existing Anatomy/Tactics/Healing roles require a future review of perceived mandatory investment, not an unrequested redesign here.</p><h2 id="mastery-and-multiple-endgames" tabindex="-1">Mastery and multiple endgames <a class="header-anchor" href="#mastery-and-multiple-endgames" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mastery and multiple endgames&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The normal skill maximum is proposed as 100, with no ordinary 120 climb. Endgame is not a universal activity and does not require 100 in every path. Knowledge, Codex/journal completion, rare techniques/schools, exploration, reputation, challenges, item/process mastery, human execution, world participation and social/economic/political progression continue beyond numeric skill growth.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Aspiration</th><th>Illustrative identities, not systems defined by this batch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Martial</td><td>Renowned duelist, monster hunter, battlefield specialist, commander</td></tr><tr><td>Craft</td><td>Master smith, master bowyer, elite alchemist, famous chef</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge / exploration</td><td>Scholar, cartographer, magical researcher, archivist</td></tr><tr><td>Economic</td><td>Merchant, workshop owner, trade network builder, real-estate specialist</td></tr><tr><td>Political / social</td><td>Councillor, faction leader, royal advisor, guild leader, tavern owner, community organizer</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These examples authorize no political, housing, mapmaking or class system. Specialization should emerge from investment, learned knowledge, practice and reputation rather than artificial class locks.</p><h2 id="reconciliation-and-open-questions" tabindex="-1">Reconciliation and open questions <a class="header-anchor" href="#reconciliation-and-open-questions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reconciliation and open questions&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/skills-and-skillcap.html#reconciliation-and-open-questions">Skill framework</a>: exact cap, gain, special models, teachers, UI and roster conversion.</li><li><a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/character-creation.html#open-questions">Creation</a>: competencies, packages, starting values, knowledge/items/reputation and future freedom.</li><li><a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/techniques-and-specialization.html#open-questions">Techniques</a>: teaching, custody, persistence and forgetting.</li><li><a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/characters/stats.html#reconciliation-and-open-questions">Stats</a>: stat limits, low-DEX cadence and skill/stat separation.</li></ul><p>Inherited behavior is not a changed balance model. Accepted server authority, <a href="/Syrolands-Public/game-design/skills-and-knowledge.html">existing skill roles</a> and the current combat slice retain their scope. No progression playtest or new Accepted ADR exists for Batch 02.</p>',21)])])}const u=a(n,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
